<Window xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        Title="ClashFlag - Clash List"
        Height="560" Width="680"
        MinHeight="320" MinWidth="460"
        WindowStartupLocation="CenterScreen"
        ResizeMode="CanResizeWithGrip"
        ShowInTaskbar="True"
        Topmost="False">
    <!--
    T-3 / ticket 1003 clash list / navigation panel (US-3). Loaded via
    pyrevit.forms.WPFWindow from clashflag_runner.py - see ClashListWindow
    there.

    No x:Class here, same reasoning as clashflag_scope_picker.xaml: pyRevit's
    WPFWindow does a XAML-only load into an already-constructed Python
    object, not a compiled code-behind partial class.

    ClashListBox is deliberately left EMPTY here, same "populate from Python"
    pattern the scope picker's HostCategoriesStack/LinksStack use: the actual
    list of clashes is only known once a run has completed, so
    ClashListWindow.__init__ adds one row per ClashResult to
    ClashListBox.Items at construction time.

    T-10 / ticket 1010 (US-8, Legend): each row item is no longer a bare
    string - ClashListWindow._build_clash_list_row (script.py) builds a small
    horizontal StackPanel per row (a colored Border "swatch" for that clash's
    category-pair color, from T-8's _color_for_category_pair, plus the same
    describe() label TextBlock this used to show as plain text) and adds THAT
    as the item. No XAML change was needed for this - ClashListBox stays
    empty here exactly as before; only what Python puts into its Items
    collection changed. SelectedIndex-based navigation (click-to-select,
    Previous/Next) is unaffected either way, since it only ever depends on
    integer index, never on what type of object each Items entry holds.

    Opened with .Show() (modeless), not .ShowDialog() like the scope picker -
    see ClashListWindow's docstring in clashflag_runner.py for why (US-4
    camera fly-to, ticket 1004, needs the Revit view interactive while this
    stays open).
    -->
    <Grid Margin="14">
        <Grid.RowDefinitions>
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
            <RowDefinition Height="*"/>
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
        </Grid.RowDefinitions>

        <TextBlock Grid.Row="0"
                   Text="All clashes found by the last run. Click a row, or use Previous/Next, to step through them one at a time."
                   TextWrapping="Wrap"
                   Margin="0,0,0,10"/>

        <ListBox Grid.Row="1" x:Name="ClashListBox" Margin="0,0,0,10"/>

        <TextBlock Grid.Row="2" x:Name="StatusText" Text="" Margin="0,0,0,8" FontWeight="Bold"/>

        <!--
        T-5 / ticket 1005 (US-5): colorize-by-category toggle. Applies to the
        WHOLE current clash_results set at once, not to whichever single
        clash is currently selected - a different kind of action from
        Previous/Next/list-click navigation, so it gets its own control
        rather than reusing the selection-changed hook. See
        ClashListWindow.colorize_checkbox_checked/_unchecked in
        clashflag_runner.py for what it actually does, and why only
        host-model elements are colorized (a researched Revit API
        limitation on linked elements - see that same docstring).
        -->
        <CheckBox Grid.Row="3" x:Name="ColorizeCheckBox"
                  Content="Colorize clashes by category"
                  Margin="0,0,0,8"
                  Checked="colorize_checkbox_checked"
                  Unchecked="colorize_checkbox_unchecked"
                  ToolTip="Color-codes each clash's HOST-side element by its host/link category pair, consistently across the current result set. Linked-side elements can't be colorized this way - Revit has no API to override an individual linked element's graphics from a host view."/>

        <!--
        T-9 / ticket 1009 (US-7, HOST-ONLY SCOPE RESTORED BY TICKET 1019):
        Isolate toggle. Applies to the CURRENT clash only (unlike
        ColorizeCheckBox above, which applies to the whole result set at
        once) - hides everything else in the active view and always also
        selects/highlights both elements, since the link-side element can't
        itself be isolated (same researched Revit API limitation as
        colorize's host-only scope). See ClashListWindow.isolate_checkbox_
        checked/_unchecked in script.py for the full lifecycle - mirrors
        ColorizeCheckBox's exact apply-on-check/clear-on-uncheck/
        clear-on-Closed pattern, but also re-applies on every
        Next/Previous/list-click navigation while checked, since "the
        current clash" itself changes underneath it. Ticket 1018 briefly
        bundled a link-side Section Box into this same checkbox; ticket 1019
        reverted that (see SectionBoxCheckBox below) - this checkbox is back
        to its original ticket-1009 host-only scope, independent of Section
        Box and Colorize.
        -->
        <CheckBox Grid.Row="4" x:Name="IsolateCheckBox"
                  Content="Isolate current clash"
                  Margin="0,0,0,8"
                  Checked="isolate_checkbox_checked"
                  Unchecked="isolate_checkbox_unchecked"
                  ToolTip="Hides everything in the active view except this clash's HOST-side element, and always also selects/highlights both elements (the link-side element can't be isolated this way - Select keeps it findable). Moving to a different clash replaces the isolation; turning this off restores full visibility. Independent of Section Box and Colorize."/>

        <!--
        T-19 / ticket 1019 (US-9): Section Box toggle - independent of
        Isolate (above) and Colorize. Crops the active 3D view tightly
        around the CURRENT clash's actual intersection region (not the two
        full clashing elements - see this ticket's fix for why ticket 1018's
        attempt, sized to the two full elements, barely cropped anything
        when one dwarfed the other), across both the host and every linked
        model, at the view-rendering level. Only works on a View3D - skipped
        with a console note otherwise. See ClashListWindow.section_box_
        checkbox_checked/_unchecked in script.py for the full lifecycle -
        mirrors IsolateCheckBox's exact apply-on-check/clear-on-uncheck/
        clear-on-Closed/re-apply-on-navigation pattern, with its own,
        completely independent state.
        -->
        <CheckBox Grid.Row="5" x:Name="SectionBoxCheckBox"
                  Content="Section Box on current clash"
                  Margin="0,0,0,8"
                  Checked="section_box_checkbox_checked"
                  Unchecked="section_box_checkbox_unchecked"
                  ToolTip="Crops the active 3D view tightly around this clash's actual intersection region, in both the host and linked model - not the two full elements' extents. Only works on a 3D view. Moving to a different clash replaces the box; turning this off restores full visibility. Independent of Isolate and Colorize."/>

        <StackPanel Grid.Row="6" Orientation="Horizontal" HorizontalAlignment="Right">
            <Button x:Name="PreviousButton" Content="Previous" Width="90" Height="28" Margin="0,0,8,0"
                    Click="previous_button_click"/>
            <Button x:Name="NextButton" Content="Next" Width="90" Height="28" Margin="0,0,8,0"
                    Click="next_button_click"/>
            <Button x:Name="CloseButton" Content="Close" Width="90" Height="28"
                    Click="close_button_click"/>
        </StackPanel>
    </Grid>
</Window>
